';\n"})}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["This allows you to write ",(0,r.jsx)(s.a,{href:"/versions/v57.0.0/sdk/contacts#getalloptions",children:(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"Contact.getAll()"})})," and read the contacts from the device, read the gyroscope sensor to detect device movement, or start the phone's camera and take photos."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h2,{id:"all-expo-sdk-packages-work-in-any-react-native-app",children:"All Expo SDK packages work in any React Native app"}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["Expo apps are React Native apps, so all Expo SDK packages work in any React Native app with the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"expo"})," package installed and configured. The easiest way to create a React Native app with support for Expo SDK packages is to use ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"create-expo-app"}),". However, you can also add Expo SDK support to an existing React Native app with the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"npx install-expo-modules"})," command."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(u.BK,{cmd:["# Create a project named my-app","$ npx create-expo-app my-app --template bare-minimum"],cmdCopy:"npx create-expo-app my-app --template bare-minimum"}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(c.u,{title:"Install Expo SDK packages in existing React Native apps",href:"/bare/installing-expo-modules",description:(0,r.jsxs)(r.Fragment,{children:["Learn more about configuring projects created with"," ",(0,r.jsx)(x.Sq,{children:"npx @react-native-community/cli@latest init"})," to Expo SDK packages."]}),Icon:l.T}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(c.u,{title:"Use libraries",description:"Learn how to install Expo SDK packages in your project.",href:"/workflow/using-libraries",Icon:l.T}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h2,{id:"using-pre-release-versions",children:"Using pre-release versions"}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["New Expo SDK versions are released three times each year. Between these releases, we publish pre-release versions of the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"expo"})," package and all of the Expo SDK packages. Pre-releases are not considered stable and should only be used if you are comfortable with the risk of encountering bugs or other issues."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h3,{id:"canary-releases",children:"Canary releases"}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["Canary releases represent a snapshot of the state of the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"main"})," branch at the time they are published. Canary package versions include ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"-canary"})," in the name, along with the date and commit hash, such as ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"57.0.0-canary-20260526-13e89ca"}),". To install the latest canary release:"]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(u.BK,{cmd:["# Install the alpha version of expo and its related packages","$ npm install expo@canary && npx expo install --fix"],cmdCopy:"npm install expo@canary && npx expo install --fix"}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["You can often use pre-release versions of individual packages with stable releases of the Expo SDK. There may occasionally be incompatibilities or other issues that arise in canary-quality releases. You may want to ",(0,r.jsx)(s.a,{href:"/../../../../../../../more/expo-cli/#configuring-dependency-validation",children:"silence dependency validation warnings"})," if you opt in to the canary package and once you have verified that it works well for your use cases."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h3,{id:"beta-releases",children:"Beta releases"}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.p,{children:["Before each Expo SDK release, we publish beta versions of the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"expo"})," package and all of the Expo SDK packages. Beta releases are considered much more stable than canary releases, and we encourage developers to try them out on their apps and share their feedback. Beta releases use the ",(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"beta"})," tag on npm and follow the instructions in the related ",(0,r.jsx)(s.a,{href:"https://expo.dev/changelog",children:"changelog"})," post."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h2,{id:"each-expo-sdk-version-depends-on-a-react-native-version",children:"Each Expo SDK version depends on a React Native version"}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(p.R3,{}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.h3,{id:"additional-information",children:"Additional information"}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(d.N,{summary:"Expo SDK policy for tracking React Native releases",children:(0,r.jsxs)(s.ul,{children:["\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.li,{children:"Each Expo SDK release targets a single React Native version. This is typically the latest stable version at the time of the release."}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.li,{children:"React Native currently has six releases per year and aims for every release to include no breaking changes."}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.li,{children:"Expo SDK releases aim to follow this cadence."}),"\n",(0,r.jsx)(s.li,{children:"Pre-release versions of the upcoming Expo SDK will include support for the latest version of React Native quickly, usually the same day it is released. A member of the Expo SDK team works on the React Native releases team for each release, and is responsible for continuously updating the React Native version in the Expo repository, verifying compatibility, and reporting regressions back to the React Native team."}),"\n"]})}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(d.N,{summary:"What if I need a change from the latest React Native version and it's not yet in an Expo SDK release?",children:[(0,r.jsx)(s.p,{children:"Expo engineers on the React Native releases team ensure that any urgent fixes are included in the React Native version used by the latest Expo SDK. If your specific fix won't be cherrypicked into an existing release, perhaps because it is not considered critical or because it involves a breaking change, then you have two options:"}),(0,r.jsxs)(s.ol,{children:["\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.li,{children:["Use ",(0,r.jsx)(s.a,{href:"https://github.com/ds300/patch-package",children:(0,r.jsx)(s.code,{children:"patch-package"})})," to pull in the fix."]}),"\n",(0,r.jsxs)(s.li,{children:["Use a ",(0,r.jsx)(s.a,{href:"/versions/v57.0.0/#using-pre-release-versions",children:"pre-release version of the Expo SDK"}